NNAAMMEE
     ggssssdd — Generic Security Services Daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ggssssdd [--dd] [--hh] [--vv] [--ss _d_i_r_-_l_i_s_t] [--cc _f_i_l_e_-_s_u_b_s_t_r_i_n_g]
          [--rr _p_r_e_f_e_r_r_e_d_-_r_e_a_l_m]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggssssdd program provides support for the kernel GSS-API implementation.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Run in debug mode.  In this mode, ggssssdd will not fork when it
             starts.

     --hh      Enable support for host-based initiator credentials.  This per‐
             mits a kerberized NFS mount to use a service principal in the de‐
             fault Kerberos 5 keytab file for access.  Such access is enabled
             via the gssname option for the mount_nfs(8) command.

     --vv      Run in verbose mode.  In this mode, ggssssdd will log activity mes‐
             sages to syslog using LOG_INFO | LOG_DAEMON or to stderr, if the
             --dd option has also been specified.  The minor status is logged as
             a decimal number, since it is actually a Kerberos return status,
             which is signed.

     --ss _d_i_r_-_l_i_s_t
             Look for an appropriate credential cache file in this list of di‐
             rectories.  The list should be full pathnames from root, sepa‐
             rated by ':' characters.  Usually this list will simply be
             "/tmp".  Without this option, ggssssdd assumes that the credential
             cache file is called /tmp/krb5cc_<uid>, where <uid> is the effec‐
             tive uid for the RPC caller.

     --cc _f_i_l_e_-_s_u_b_s_t_r_i_n_g
             Set a file-substring for the credential cache file names.  Only
             files with this substring embedded in their names will be se‐
             lected as candidates when --ss has been specified.  If not speci‐
             fied, it defaults to "krb5cc_".

     --rr _p_r_e_f_e_r_r_e_d_-_r_e_a_l_m
             Use Kerberos credentials for this realm when searching for cre‐
             dentials in directories specified with --ss.  If not specified, the
             default Kerberos realm will be used.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/krb5.keytab  Contains Kerberos service principals which may be used
                       as credentials by kernel GSS-API services.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ggssssdd utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     gssapi(3), syslog(3), mount_nfs(8)

